items: js-char-type-test-cannot-tell-Char-from-String
paths: file:specs/type-ascription-matrix.md file:v2/src/Runtime.scala file:v1/runtime/backend/js/src/main/scala/scalascript/codegen/JsGen.scala file:v1/runtime/backend/js/src/main/scala/scalascript/codegen/JsGenCpsCodegen.scala file:v1/runtime/backend/js/BUGS.md file:tests/e2e/char-type-test.sh
scope: |
  `case _: Char` cannot be told from `case _: String`. Measured on a FRESH build, four lanes, one
  probe (kind('c') / kind("c") / kind(99) / kind(s.charAt(0))):

      --v1 (reference)  Char  String  Int  Char     <- correct on all four
      bin/ssc           Int   String  Int  Int
      --bytecode        Int   String  Int  Int
      run-js            String String Int  other

  TWO DIFFERENT CAUSES, and only one of them is the representation gap the entry describes:
    - native/bytecode: `CharV extends IntV`, so a Char falls into the IntV arm of `__isTag__` and
      answers Int. The TYPE EXISTS -- CharV became a distinct class in f39448c96 -- so this is one
      arm, ordered before IntV.
    - js: a Char RESULT is a `_Char` box and there is simply no "Char" arm in the type-test mapping,
      so it answered `other`. Also one arm (`_isChar`).
    - js LITERAL: genuinely the representation gap. `'c'` is a plain one-char string, so it stays
      String, and `case _: String` on a real one-char string must keep working.

  specs/type-ascription-matrix.md classifies BOTH lanes as "a missing TYPE -- not fixable in a type
  test at all". That was true when written (2026-07-31) and is now STALE for native: the census
  predates CharV. Updating the census is part of this claim -- a spec that says "not fixable" about
  a one-line fix is worse than no spec.
next: rebuild, re-measure all four lanes, then gate + corpus
